<BaseLayout title="Rainbows, Adele and Zzyzx — Huanyu Chen"
  description="Labor Day weekend 2023: I-15 to Las Vegas through a storm and a double rainbow, Adele at Caesars Palace, the Sphere before it opened, and a detour to Zzyzx on the way home.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · September 2023</p>
  <h1>Rainbows, Adele and Zzyzx</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">A Labor Day weekend in Las Vegas, driven both ways on I-15: storm and sunset going up, floodwater and an oasis coming down.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>3 days · September 2–4, 2023</strong><br />
    Los Angeles → I-15 → Las Vegas → I-15 → Zzyzx → Los Angeles<br />
    About 550 miles of interstate · two rainbows at once · one Adele concert · one turn of the High Roller ·
    one giant LED ball, four weeks before its opening night · one road with the last name in the atlas
  </div>

  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-overview.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-overview.jpg'} alt="Map of the weekend: from Los Angeles up I-15 past a double rainbow to the Las Vegas Strip, then back down I-15 past a flooded dry lake to Zzyzx and home." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>The whole weekend: up I-15 on Saturday, down it on Monday.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Some trips are built around a place. This one was built around a ticket. Adele's Las Vegas residency,
    <em>Weekends with Adele</em>, was in its second season, and we had seats for the Saturday night of Labor Day weekend.
    Everything else (the drive, the Strip, the wheel, the strange little oasis on the way home) grew around that one
    evening.
  </p>
  <p>
    Las Vegas from Los Angeles is the most ordinary road trip in Southern California. It is about 270 miles of I-15,
    four hours if the traffic is kind, and on a holiday weekend it almost never is. Half of Los Angeles seems to make the
    drive on Friday or Saturday and the other half makes it back on Monday. Nobody expects much of the drive itself.
    This time it delivered a storm, two rainbows at once, a desert sunset, a lakebed that had turned back into a lake, and a place called Zzyzx.
    As usual, the story below is put back together from the camera roll, its timestamps and its locations. Where the
    phone was quiet, so am I.
  </p>

  <h2>Day 1: Up I-15, into the storm</h2>

  <h3>Half a mile in forty minutes</h3>
  <p>
    The first photo of the trip was taken at 5:51 on Saturday afternoon, a few miles past Primm, the cluster of casinos
    that sits right on the Nevada state line. Until then the phone had stayed in a pocket, and then the desert decided to put on a show. A ceiling of slate-colored
    cloud sat low over the mountains west of the freeway, with a strip of pale blue sky showing underneath. Wisps of
    rain cloud were snagged on the ridges like smoke.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0902.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0902.jpg'} alt="Map of the route: Day 1: I-15 north from near Primm through the storm and sunset to the Strip, ending at the Colosseum." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 1: the last fifty miles into Las Vegas took two and a half hours.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    We had plenty of time to look at it, because we weren't going anywhere. According to the phone's GPS, between
    5:51 and 6:30 we moved a little over half a mile. Northbound I-15 had become a parking lot of the familiar
    holiday kind: sedans, pickups, RVs and eighteen-wheelers all pointed at Las Vegas, brake lights glowing red under
    a dark sky. In the desert, a traffic jam has a special quality. There is nothing to look at but the sky,
    so you look at the sky.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'storm-mountains.jpg'} alt="Dark storm clouds over a jagged desert mountain ridge beside the freeway, with wisps of cloud caught on the slopes." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The view from a standstill, just past the Nevada line.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Then the rain came, and right after it the reward. At 6:30, with the sun breaking through somewhere behind us,
    a rainbow came down out of the gray and planted itself in the desert just east of the freeway. Then a second, fainter one appeared
    outside it. A double rainbow happens when sunlight is reflected twice inside each raindrop instead of once. The
    second bow is always wider and dimmer, and its colors run in the opposite order, red on the inside. The band of
    sky between the two is noticeably darker than the rest, an effect with a name: Alexander's dark band. The end of the
    bow seemed to stand on the pale floor of Roach Dry Lake, behind a row of power poles, close enough to see where it
    met the ground, which a rainbow is not supposed to let you do.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'double-rainbow.jpg'} alt="A rainbow arcing down out of gray storm clouds onto the desert beside the freeway, with a fainter second bow outside it." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Two bows, if you look closely, at 6:30 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'rainbow-lakebed.jpg'} alt="The foot of a rainbow standing on a pale dry lakebed behind power poles and desert scrub." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Where the rainbow touched down: Roach Dry Lake.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Somewhere around a quarter to seven, the jam let go, and the light did something else. The cloud deck lifted
    off the western mountains and the low sun slid underneath it, so the whole world turned gold from the bottom up:
    gold on the ridgelines, gold on the concrete median, gold on the sides of the trucks passing in the next lane. The
    wipers were still going. The phone, pressed to the window at highway speed, recorded it the way phones do,
    with a wiper blade swinging through the middle of the frame and raindrops on the glass. I've kept one of those,
    because that is exactly what it looked like.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'golden-light.jpg'} alt="Golden evening light under a low gray cloud deck over desert mountains, seen past a concrete freeway median." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The sun gets under the clouds, 6:49 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'wiper.jpg'} alt="A blurred windshield wiper sweeping across a view of desert mountains with low clouds and power lines." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Still raining, still beautiful.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Past Jean and Sloan, the desert opens out and the freeway climbs gently toward the Las Vegas Valley. The storm was
    moving off to the east, and as it went it left one more piece of rainbow standing upright over the mountains:
    just a short stub of color, no arc, hanging above a pink-lit cloud bank on the ridge. The road ahead was wet and
    shining and, for the first time in hours, nearly empty.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'rainbow-fragment.jpg'} alt="A short vertical fragment of rainbow above a desert mountain ridge, over a wide wet freeway." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A last piece of rainbow near Sloan, 7:00 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    And then, as if the evening hadn't done enough, the sunset. In the last fifteen minutes before the city, the
    clouds broke into long ragged bands, and the sun lit their undersides orange and salmon against a sky that was still
    clear blue overhead. Distant peaks went black in silhouette. By the time we reached the southern edge of
    the valley, it was palm trees against the afterglow over the freeway wall, the clouds edged in fire. Then the
    first casino towers rose ahead, and Las Vegas began.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sunset-mountains.jpg'} alt="A dramatic sunset with dark clouds lit orange from below over a silhouetted desert mountain range." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sunset over the mountains, 7:01 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sunset-palms.jpg'} alt="Bands of orange and gray sunset clouds over silhouetted palm trees and power lines." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Palms at the edge of the city, 7:08 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Weekends with Adele</h3>
  <p>
    An hour later we were in our seats in <strong>The Colosseum at Caesars Palace</strong>. The Colosseum was built in
    2003 for Celine Dion, whose residency there more or less invented the modern Las Vegas residency. Instead of
    touring, a star stays put and the audience flies in. It seats a little over four thousand, which is huge for a
    theater and tiny for an Adele concert, and that is the point. Adele's residency had a rocky start. She called off
    the original opening in January 2022 the day before the first show, saying it wasn't ready. It finally opened in
    November 2022, and it would run, on and off, for two years.
  </p>
  <p>
    The stage at 8:25 was nearly bare: a white grand piano, a pianist, two great columns of green light leaning in
    toward each other like the sides of a cathedral window, and Adele, in a long black gown, microphone in hand,
    talking to the room. The residency was known for that as much as for the songs: long, chatty stretches between
    numbers, the stage kept simple so that the voice does the work.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'adele-stage.jpg'} alt="Adele in a long black gown on a dark stage beside a white grand piano and its pianist, framed by two tall beams of green light." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Colosseum, 8:25 pm: a piano, two beams of green light, and Adele.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The show ran about an hour and a half. The phone recorded parts of it, and those clips are better left
    in the camera roll. What they show is a show built mostly on her voice, with a few big gestures: at one point
    columns of fire shot up along the front of the stage. The only physical
    souvenirs were the ones from the merchandise stand: a black <em>Weekends with Adele</em> T-shirt with her portrait
    printed in cream and orange, a navy bag with ADELE spelled out down its side, and a fan of Polaroid-style photo
    cards. Late that night they were laid out in a row and photographed, as souvenirs are.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'adele-merch.jpg'} alt="A black Weekends with Adele T-shirt with a portrait print, a navy ADELE bag, and a scatter of Polaroid-style photo cards." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>What came home from the Colosseum.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Day 2: The Strip by daylight</h2>
  <p>
    Las Vegas by day is a different city: hot and bright, and slightly embarrassed, like a party venue the
    morning after. Sunday was the day to look at it. The storm had left the sky full of big, clean cumulus clouds,
    and everything had the scrubbed look that desert air gets after rain.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0903.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0903.jpg'} alt="Map of the route: Day 2: lunch across from the Wynn, bubble tea, the Sphere by day, and the High Roller at night." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 2: the north end of the Strip by day, the High Roller by night.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    The first photo of the morning, at 11:29, is the one everybody in Las Vegas was taking that summer:
    <strong>the Sphere</strong>. It sits a block east of the Strip behind the Venetian, a ball 366 feet tall and 516
    feet wide, and that September it was finished but not yet open. Its outer skin, which the builders call the Exosphere,
    is covered in about 1.2 million LED pucks, which makes it the largest video screen on Earth wrapped around the largest
    spherical building. It had been switched on that July 4th, and for two months it had been showing off: an
    eyeball, a basketball, the moon. That morning, seen over the cones and new paving on Sands Avenue,
    it was wearing deep blue stripes that curved around it like the seams of a beach ball.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sphere-day.jpg'} alt="The Sphere, a huge round building covered in curving blue LED stripes, behind trees and a construction-coned road under a cloudy sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Sphere at 11:29 am, four weeks before opening night.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Lunch across from the Wynn</h3>
  <p>
    Lunch was an Italian place one floor up on the Strip, with a wall of glass looking straight across Las Vegas
    Boulevard at the Wynn. The Wynn tower, opened in 2005, is a single fifty-story curve clad in bronze glass, and at
    noon it glows a coppery brown that changes with every cloud. The restaurant's name was lettered on the window,
    so from inside it reads backwards across the view.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'wynn-window.jpg'} alt="The curved bronze-glass tower of the Wynn seen through a tall restaurant window, with reversed lettering on the glass and traffic on the boulevard below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Wynn, through the restaurant window.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The food deserves its own paragraph. On the table: a braised shank standing upright on its bone in a dark,
    glossy sauce over a bed of spinach and mashed potatoes, a plate of pasta under a nest of crispy fried onions, and
    a third dish in a golden broth. The meal ended, as it should in an Italian restaurant, with tiramisu: a neat block
    of cocoa-dusted layers, shards of dark chocolate on top, and a zigzag of chocolate sauce across the plate.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lunch-shank.jpg'} alt="A braised shank on the bone in dark sauce over spinach, with pasta and another dish behind it on a white tablecloth." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Lunch, mostly shank.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'tiramisu.jpg'} alt="A slice of tiramisu with chocolate shavings on top and a zigzag of chocolate sauce on the plate." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>And tiramisu.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Below the window, the boulevard was its usual self. Las Vegas has a peculiar form of advertising: trucks whose whole
    side is a glowing billboard, which do nothing all day but drive slowly up and down the Strip. Two of them rolled past
    in the noon traffic, both selling the same show, both featuring a gentleman who had misplaced his shirt. Across the
    street, the Wynn's gardens and palms looked cool and green, which in a Las Vegas September is an illusion.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'strip-billboards.jpg'} alt="Las Vegas Boulevard seen from above through glass, with traffic, palms, and two trucks carrying lit-up billboards for a show." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Noon traffic on Las Vegas Boulevard, billboard trucks included.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The afternoon was slow. In mid-afternoon came a bubble-tea break, in the tall clear cups of a shop called
    Pink Pink: a matcha drink swirled green and white in stripes, under a cap of matcha cream, and a strawberry drink
    with a white foam top and red popping pearls at the bottom.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'pink-pink.jpg'} alt="Two tall bubble-tea cups, one striped green and white with matcha, one pink strawberry with a foam top and red pearls." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Matcha and strawberry, 2:31 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>The Sphere, again</h3>
  <p>
    The Strip's north end is linked by pedestrian bridges, so walking it means climbing escalators, crossing above the
    traffic and coming back down. From the bridges at a quarter past three, the Sphere kept appearing between
    things: behind a row of palms and the sandstone walls of the Venetian's shops, and then, from a bridge over the road,
    whole. It had changed its skin since the morning. Now it was a planet, mottled purple, rust and brown like a
    gas giant, with the diamond grid of the LED panels showing faintly through. Below it, the traffic waited in a long
    line of orange construction barrels. It is hard to describe how odd the Sphere looks in person. It is far too big
    to be a ball and far too round to be a building, and your eyes keep trying to fix the scale and failing.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sphere-palms.jpg'} alt="The dark Sphere rising behind palm trees and the walls of a shopping arcade, seen from a pedestrian bridge." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Sphere, peeking over the palms.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sphere-planet.jpg'} alt="The Sphere displaying a mottled purple and brown planet surface, above a road lined with orange construction barrels." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>An afternoon as a planet.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The photos go quiet for the next few hours, and I'll leave those hours alone. Sunday ended in the air.
  </p>

  <h3>Thirty minutes on the High Roller</h3>
  <p>
    At 550 feet, the <strong>High Roller</strong> at the LINQ was the tallest observation wheel in the world from its
    opening in 2014 until Dubai built a taller one in 2021. It turns so slowly you can't see it move. A single
    revolution takes about half an hour, and the cabins never stop; you step aboard while it creeps past the platform. Each of its
    28 cabins is a glass capsule big enough for a few dozen people, air-conditioned and fitted with screens. The first
    clip from our cabin is from 8:22, as it started climbing out of the LINQ's lights into the dark.
  </p>
  <p>
    From the top, the Strip turns out to be a much smaller and much brighter thing than it seems from the ground. To
    the north, the Venetian's long gold façade was lit up like a stage set, with the red sign of Harrah's below and the dark grid of the city running out to the mountains, a sheet of
    tiny lights. The window glass, the cabin lights and the long exposures of a phone at night all argued with each
    other. Most of the photos show some reflection, a glow, a smear of light, but they are honest.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'highroller-venetian.jpg'} alt="Night view from the High Roller over the illuminated Venetian and neighboring towers, with the city lights stretching to the horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Looking north from the High Roller: the Venetian and beyond.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Looking south, the view was the Strip proper. In the foreground, the next cabin hung in the air like a glass
    Christmas ornament. Below it were the pink neon flamingo of the Flamingo and a gold-lit block of hotel towers along the
    boulevard, with the blue-lit glass of the Cosmopolitan beyond. From this height the whole glittering thing looked
    like a model of itself.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'highroller-strip.jpg'} alt="Night view south over the Las Vegas Strip from a High Roller cabin, with another glass cabin in the foreground and brightly lit casino towers." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The view south, with a neighboring cabin for scale.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The best surprise of the ride was to the east. At 8:45, near the top, the Sphere had stopped pretending to be a
    planet and was advertising its opening act: U2:UV, <em>Achtung Baby</em> live at Sphere. In a field of deep
    blue, a red-and-white target, the band's name in glowing neon letters and the word LIVE, the whole thing lighting
    up the dark blocks around it like a second moon. Four weeks later, on September 29th, U2 would open the building.
    That night, though, it was just a giant ball of light talking to the sky.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sphere-u2.jpg'} alt="The Sphere at night glowing deep blue, displaying U2:UV in neon-style letters over a red-and-white target, seen from above." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Sphere at 8:45 pm, advertising the show that would open it.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Day 3: Floodwater, and the end of the alphabet</h2>
  <p>
    Monday was Labor Day, and on Labor Day everybody leaves Las Vegas at once. We started with the most Las Vegas
    meal there is: lunch at the <strong>Bacchanal Buffet</strong> at Caesars Palace, where plates go back to the
    stations as often as you like. The camera roll records one round: on one plate, carved meats and fried rice, and on
    the other, a small sampler of tiny dishes in glass cubes, sushi, wedges of pineapple and melon.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0904.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0904.jpg'} alt="Map of the route: Day 3: from the Bacchanal Buffet down I-15 past a flooded dry lake and Halloran Springs to Zzyzx." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Day 3: the long way home, with one detour.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'buffet.jpg'} alt="Two buffet plates on a wooden table: one piled with meats and fried rice, one with small dishes in glass cubes, sushi and fruit." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Round one at the Bacchanal Buffet.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>A lake in the desert</h3>
  <p>
    By 3:30 we were back at the state line, just into California, and the desert had changed since Saturday.
    Beside the freeway lies <strong>Ivanpah Dry Lake</strong>, a flat bed of baked clay that is dry nearly every day of
    the year. It wasn't dry now. Late summer 2023 had been wild out here: Tropical Storm Hilary had soaked the Mojave two
    weeks earlier, and the storms we had driven through on Saturday added more. The lakebed was a sheet of shining
    water from the shoulder to the mountains, with a fence line and the tops of the bushes poking through, and an
    orange traffic cone standing in the shallows like it had been posted there on purpose.
  </p>
  <p>
    Beyond the water, past the high-voltage towers, a point of white light burned on the slope as if a star had landed
    there. That is the <strong>Ivanpah Solar Electric Generating System</strong>, opened in 2014: three towers
    about 450 feet tall, each surrounded by a field of tens of thousands of flat mirrors that track the sun and aim its
    light at a boiler at the top. The boiler gets so hot it glows. From I-15 the towers look like lighthouses that
    someone forgot to turn off in daytime, and with a lake in front of them they were twice as strange.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ivanpah-flood.jpg'} alt="A flooded dry lakebed glittering beside the freeway, with power towers and a glowing solar tower at the foot of the mountains beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Ivanpah Dry Lake, not dry, with the solar towers behind it.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ivanpah-cone.jpg'} alt="Muddy floodwater beside the freeway with an orange traffic cone in it, the bright lakebed and a glowing solar tower in the distance." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A traffic cone, keeping an eye on the lake.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Then came the traffic. Southbound I-15 on Labor Day afternoon is a famous ordeal, and it was true to form: according to the
    timestamps, it took an hour and forty minutes to cover the thirty-odd miles from the state line to Halloran
    Springs, past the long downhill toward Baker. The desert at least was handsome in the late light: a dark volcanic
    cone beside the road, and range after range fading into haze toward the south.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'halloran-springs.jpg'} alt="Desert scrub and a dark rocky hill beside the freeway near Halloran Springs, with hazy mountain ranges in the distance." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Near Halloran Springs, 5:11 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Zzyzx</h3>
  <p>
    A few miles past Baker there is an exit that every Las Vegas driver has seen and almost nobody takes:
    <strong>Zzyzx Road</strong>. We took it. The name, pronounced roughly "ZYE-zix", was invented in 1944 by
    Curtis Howe Springer, a radio evangelist from Los Angeles. He claimed the land around a set of springs on the edge
    of Soda Dry Lake and built a health resort there, the Zzyzx Mineral Springs and Health Spa, with a pond, rows of palms and
    guest buildings, and promoted it and his health tonics on his radio show. He chose the name so that it would be the
    last word in the English language. In 1974 the federal government, which had never agreed that the land was his,
    evicted him. Since 1976 the place has been the <strong>Desert Studies Center</strong>, a field station run
    by the California State University and set inside the Mojave National Preserve.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'zzyzx-sign.jpg'} alt="A National Park Service sign reading Welcome to the Desert Studies Center, with a photo of palms reflected in a pond and panels on Zzyzx history." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The welcome sign, with the history in small print.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    We arrived at a quarter to six, after a few miles of road out across the desert, and the place was empty and
    very still. Springer's buildings are still there, low and plain, along the foot of a rocky hill. The light was
    already going gold on the hilltop while the ground lay in blue shadow. The shaggy palms look like California fan palms, the
    only palm native to the state, left to grow the way they do in the wild. Their dead fronds
    hang down in a thick shaggy skirt to the ground, so each one looks less like a tree than like a large, patient
    animal in a hairy coat.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'zzyzx-hill.jpg'} alt="The low buildings of the Desert Studies Center and a pond at the foot of a rocky hill, the hilltop lit gold by the evening sun." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The old resort, under its hill.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'zzyzx-palm.jpg'} alt="A California fan palm with a thick shaggy skirt of dead fronds, against a deep blue evening sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A fan palm in its full skirt.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    A whole grove of them stands on the gravel beside the road, behind a low rope fence: a dozen shaggy shapes in a loose
    crowd, like a herd that has stopped to graze.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'zzyzx-palms.jpg'} alt="A grove of shaggy fan palms on bare gravel behind a rope fence, with the rocky hill behind them." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The grove.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The pond is <strong>Lake Tuendae</strong>, and it is the reason to come. Springer dug it as part of his resort.
    Today it is a refuge for the Mohave tui chub, a small, endangered fish that once lived throughout the ancient
    Mojave River system and now survives in only a handful of places like this one. At ten to six on Labor Day, there
    was no wind at all. The water was a perfect mirror, and a line of tall, skinny palms along the far bank stood on top of its
    own reflection, upside down in the blue, with the bare mountains and the white floor of Soda Lake beyond.
    After three days of neon, it was the quietest place of the whole trip, and maybe the most beautiful.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lake-tuendae.jpg'} alt="A line of tall palms reflected in the still water of Lake Tuendae, framed by reeds and an overhanging palm frond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Lake Tuendae, 5:51 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'lake-tuendae-2.jpg'} alt="The same row of palms mirrored in the glassy pond under a clear blue sky, with desert mountains beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Two minutes later, not a ripple.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The last photo was taken at 5:53. After that it was back up Zzyzx Road to the interstate and the rest of the
    long Labor Day crawl home to Los Angeles, which the camera roll, wisely, does not record.
  </p>

  <h2>The shortest road trip</h2>
  <p>
    Las Vegas is the road trip everyone in Los Angeles has done, and that is why it is easy to stop seeing the road.
    I-15 is the thing between you and the show. This weekend it refused to be ignored. It gave us a storm and a
    double rainbow on the way up, a sky on fire at the edge of the city, and on the way back a dry lake full of water
    and a solar plant glowing behind it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The city did its part, too. We heard one of the great voices of the age in a room built for residencies, and a city of lights
    from 550 feet up. We caught the Sphere in its last month as a mystery, before anyone had been inside. But if I had
    to pick one picture from the weekend, it would not be any of those. It would be a row of palms standing on its own
    reflection in a pond at Zzyzx, at the end of a road named for the end of the alphabet, five miles from the
    traffic. The best stop of the trip was the one we almost drove past.
  </p>

